       The kernel system call has a third argument:

           iinntt ggeettccppuu((uunnssiiggnneedd iinntt **_c_p_u,, uunnssiiggnneedd iinntt **_n_o_d_e,,
                      ssttrruucctt ggeettccppuu__ccaacchhee **_t_c_a_c_h_e));;

       The _t_c_a_c_h_e argument is unused since Linux 2.6.24,  and  (when  invoking
       the system call directly) should be specified as NULL, unless portabil‐
       ity to Linux 2.6.23 or earlier is required.

       In  Linux 2.6.23 and earlier, if the _t_c_a_c_h_e argument was non‐NULL, then
       it specified a pointer to a  caller‐allocated  buffer  in  thread‐local
       storage that was used to provide a caching mechanism for ggeettccppuu().  Use
       of  the  cache could speed ggeettccppuu() calls, at the cost that there was a
       very small chance that the returned information would be out  of  date.
       The  caching  mechanism was considered to cause problems when migrating
       threads between CPUs, and so the argument is now ignored.

NNOOTTEESS
       Linux makes a best effort to make this call as fast as  possible.   (On
       some architectures, this is done via an implementation in the vvddssoo(7).)
       The  intention  of  ggeettccppuu() is to allow programs to make optimizations
       with per‐CPU data or for NUMA optimization.

